; =====================================================================
; bignums.cddl
;
; VERBATIM.  The twelve lines between the BEGIN and END markers below are
; draft-ietf-cbor-serialization-08's own CDDL, reproduced byte-for-byte
; from Appendix F ("CDDL for Big Numbers"), lines 1811-1822 of the cached
; text rendering (.cache/draft-ietf-cbor-serialization.txt, sha256
; a612e6c4f674da61b1c888431a06dca13457aaea53c1cd86c8b50f8999b47112),
; their three-space page margin and the two-space alignment inside
; `pplus-biguint  =` included.  Introduced in the source by the sentence
; "The following CDDL can be used:".  Nothing here is ours; the derived
; artifacts in this directory are named `*.derived.*`.
; =====================================================================
;
; WHAT APPENDIX F IS FOR.  It is the draft's answer to a problem it
; creates: Section 4.1 rule 5 merges the big number tags into the integer
; space ("If a value can be encoded using major type 0 or 1, then it MUST
; be encoded with major type 0 or 1, never as a big number"), and no CDDL
; prelude type describes that merged space.  Appendix F says so in the
; first sentence - "The types bigint and biguint in the CDDL Standard
; Prelude (Appendix D of [RFC8610]) do NOT describe the big numbers
; described in this document or in Section 3.4.3 of [STD94], not even for
; general serialization" - and then concedes the point for its own rules
; too: "The types integer and unsigned can be used, but note that they do
; not fully express the rules that govern the choice between major types
; 0 and 1 and the big number tags.  CDDL-described protocols SHOULD use
; integer and unsigned and in prose state that these values correspond to
; either Section 4 of this document or Section 3.4.3 of [STD94]."
;
; So the draft's own recommendation to a protocol author is NOT to use
; the rules below: it is to write `integer` / `unsigned` and carry the
; rest in prose.  These rules exist to show the shape of the thing.
;
; WHAT IT DOES NOT CONSTRAIN, and there is a lot of it:
;
;   * Leading zeros.  Section 4.1 rule 5 bullet 1 is "Leading zeros MUST
;     NOT be encoded"; `bstr .size (8..bnmax)` constrains the length of
;     the byte string and says nothing about its first byte.  The two
;     general-only vectors h'c24e0000ffff...' and h'c34c00000001...'
;     (Appendix L.2, positive_bignum.edn and negative_bignum.edn) both
;     satisfy this CDDL and both violate rule 5.
;   * The integer/big-number choice itself, in the other direction.
;     `pplus-biguint = #0 / pplus-tag2bstr` is a plain type choice, so
;     any value matches it in both arms; the rule that the major-type-0
;     arm MUST be taken whenever it can represent the value is not in
;     the grammar.  See prefp-encode.derived.pseudocode.txt, which is
;     where this library states it as a procedure.
;   * The upper bound.  `bnmax = 1000` is admitted in the source's own
;     comment to be a fiction: "big number byte strings are of unlimited
;     size, but that can't be expressed in CDDL, so a limit of 1,000".
;   * Anything at all about tags 2 and 3 appearing in *general*
;     serialization, where both the leading zeros and the unification
;     are permitted.  The `pplus-` prefix is load-bearing.
;
; AND ONE DEFECT, recorded here and not repaired, because this file is
; the source's bytes.  `.size (8..bnmax)` admits an 8-byte content, and
; no 8-byte content can occur in preferred-plus serialization.  An
; 8-byte tag 2 content without leading zeros has a value of at most
; 2^64-1, which major type 0 represents, so rule 5 bullet 2 forbids it;
; one with leading zeros is forbidden by bullet 1.  The same argument
; bounds tag 3 by -1-(2^64-1) = -2^64, which major type 1 represents.
; The smallest content either tag can legally carry is 9 bytes, which is
; exactly what the draft's own preferred-plus vector in Appendix L.2
; shows: negative_bignum.edn's h'c349010000000000000000' is tag 3 with a
; 9-byte content, and its note says it is "the value closest that can be
; represented as a big number, not a type 1 integer".  The bound should
; read (9..bnmax).  Reported, not corrected: see README.md and
; ../examples/vectors.yaml.
; =====================================================================

; ---- BEGIN VERBATIM (draft-ietf-cbor-serialization-08, Appendix F) ----
   ; big number byte strings are of unlimited size, but that
   ; can't be expressed in CDDL, so a limit of 1,000
   bnmax = 1000
   bnbstr = bstr .size (8..bnmax)

   pplus-tag2bstr = #6.2(bnbstr)
   pplus-biguint  = #0 / pplus-tag2bstr

   pplus-tag3bstr = #6.3(bnbstr)
   pplus-bignint  = #1 / pplus-tag3bstr

   pplus-bigint = pplus-biguint / pplus-bignint
; ---- END VERBATIM ----
